{"claims":[{"excerpt":"Yellowstonewolven in een netwerk van voedselrelaties","claim":"Yellowstone wolves are part of a network of food relationships.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Many other animals benefit from wolf kills.","narrative":"hun prooien voeden ook andere dieren","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source describes wolf kills feeding multiple other animals, supporting this concise characterization.","materiality":"Frames the piece’s central idea."},{"excerpt":"Yellowstonewolven leven in sociale familiegroepen.","claim":"Yellowstone wolves live in social family groups.","category":"entity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The pack is a complex social family, with older members (often the alpha male and alpha female) and subordinates, each having individual personality traits and roles within the pack.","narrative":"Yellowstonewolven als sociale familiegroepen","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"This accurately summarizes the source’s description of wolf packs.","materiality":"Provides context for the piece’s account of wolf social behavior."},{"excerpt":"Ze jagen samen","claim":"Yellowstone wolves hunt together.","category":"other","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Research in Yellowstone since reintroduction has highlighted the adaptive value of social living in wolves – from cooperative care of offspring, group hunting of large prey, defense of territory and prey carcasses, and even survival benefits to infirmed individuals.","narrative":"ze jagen in groepen","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source explicitly identifies group hunting of large prey.","materiality":"Describes a central example of social behavior."},{"excerpt":"zorgen voor hun jongen.","claim":"Yellowstone wolves care for their offspring.","category":"other","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Research in Yellowstone since reintroduction has highlighted the adaptive value of social living in wolves – from cooperative care of offspring, group hunting of large prey, defense of territory and prey carcasses, and even survival benefits to infirmed individuals.","narrative":"zorg voor jongen","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source identifies cooperative care of offspring as a benefit of social living.","materiality":"Describes a central example of social behavior."},{"excerpt":"Ook een gedode prooi wordt onderdeel van bredere voedselrelaties","claim":"A wolf-killed prey animal becomes part of broader food relationships.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Many other animals benefit from wolf kills.","narrative":"hun prooien voeden ook andere dieren","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source supports this interpretation by describing other animals benefiting from wolf kills.","materiality":"Connects predation to food relationships beyond wolves."},{"excerpt":"raven, coyotes, beren en ongewervelden kunnen ervan eten.","claim":"Ravens, coyotes, bears, and invertebrates can eat the remains of wolf kills.","category":"entity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Coyotes arrive soon after, waiting nearby until the wolves are sated. Bears will attempt to chase the wolves away, and are usually successful. Many other animals—from eagles to invertebrates—consume the remains.","narrative":"dieren van raven en coyotes tot beren en ongewervelden","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source names these animals as benefiting from or consuming wolf-kill remains; it does not imply they all feed in the same way or at the same time.","materiality":"Specific examples substantiate the food-relationship claim."},{"excerpt":"De prooisamenstelling laat eveneens zien dat predatiepatronen kunnen veranderen.","claim":"Changes in prey composition show that predation patterns can change.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The discovery of these changes emphasizes the importance of long-term monitoring to understand predator-prey dynamics. Changes in wolf predation patterns and impacts on prey species like elk are inextricably linked to other factors","narrative":"De veranderde samenstelling van hun prooi in verschillende perioden maakt concreet dat predatiepatronen veranderen","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source describes changes in prey composition and changes in wolf predation patterns over time; the sentence presents the former as an example of the latter.","materiality":"Introduces the piece’s concrete example of changing predation."},{"excerpt":"Onder de vroege-winterprooien verschoof tussen 1995–2000 en 2001–2007 de verhouding tussen elandkalveren en volwassen mannelijke elanden.","claim":"Among early-winter prey, the relative proportions of elk calves and adult male elk shifted between 1995–2000 and 2001–2007.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"From 1995 to 2000, in early winter, elk calves comprised 50% of wolf prey, and bull elk comprised 25%. That ratio reversed from 2001 to 2007, indicating changes in prey vulnerability and availability.","narrative":"de verschuiving in de verhouding tussen elandkalveren en volwassen mannelijke elanden onder de vroege-winterprooien in 1995–2000 en 2001–2007","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source gives the periods and says the ratio reversed. The piece accurately reports a shift without adding a direction or magnitude.","materiality":"This is the piece’s specific empirical example."},{"excerpt":"De veranderingen hielden verband met de beschikbaarheid en kwetsbaarheid van prooien","claim":"The changes were related to prey availability and vulnerability.","category":"cause","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"That ratio reversed from 2001 to 2007, indicating changes in prey vulnerability and availability.","narrative":"de bron verbindt die veranderingen met de beschikbaarheid en kwetsbaarheid van prooien","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"This closely follows the source’s account of what the ratio reversal indicates.","materiality":"Qualifies how the source interprets the observed shift."},{"excerpt":"ze zijn niet aan één oorzaak toe te schrijven.","claim":"The changes cannot be attributed to a single cause.","category":"cause","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Consequently, changes in prey selection and kill rates through time result from complex interactions among these factors.","narrative":"Schrijf de verschuiving in prooisamenstelling niet toe aan één oorzaak.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source attributes changes over time to complex interactions among factors, not a single cause.","materiality":"Prevents an oversimplified causal account."},{"excerpt":"De effecten van predatie op elanden vallen bovendien samen met andere factoren, waaronder andere roofdieren, het beheer van hoefdieren buiten het park en het weer.","claim":"The effects of predation on elk occur amid other factors, including other predators, ungulate management outside the park, and weather.","category":"cause","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Changes in wolf predation patterns and impacts on prey species like elk are inextricably linked to other factors, such as other predators, management of ungulates outside the park, and weather (e.g. drought, winter severity).","narrative":"veranderingen in predatie en de effecten op elanden samenvallen met andere factoren, waaronder andere roofdieren, beheer van hoefdieren buiten het park en het weer","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The named factors and their relationship to changes in predation and elk impacts match the source. “Vallen samen” does not overstate the source’s causal account.","materiality":"Central to the piece’s emphasis on multiple influences."},{"excerpt":"De wolven maken zo deel uit van sociale en voedselrelaties binnen een samenspel van invloeden","claim":"Wolves are part of social and food relationships within an interplay of influences.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The pack is a complex social family","narrative":"wolven maken deel uit van sociale en voedselrelaties","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source separately describes wolf packs as social families, wolf kills feeding other animals, and predation changes interacting with other factors. The piece synthesizes these supported points.","materiality":"Summarizes the piece’s framing."},{"excerpt":"niet van een systeem waarin zij alleen de uitkomsten voor elanden bepalen.","claim":"Wolves do not alone determine outcomes for elk.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Changes in wolf predation patterns and impacts on prey species like elk are inextricably linked to other factors, such as other predators, management of ungulates outside the park, and weather (e.g. drought, winter severity).","narrative":"niet bij wolven als enige bepalende kracht","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source identifies multiple factors linked to changes in wolf predation and impacts on elk, supporting the claim that wolves are not the sole determinant.","materiality":"Limits the causal scope of the piece’s conclusion."}]}

